Package: stepwedgepower
Type: Package
Title: Simulation-Based Power Evaluation for Stepped-Wedge Cluster Randomized Trials
Version: 0.3.0
Authors@R: c(
    person(given = "Lin (Amanda)", family = "Li", role = c("aut", "cre"),
           email = "amandali14124277@gmail.com"),
    person(given = "Florin", family = "Vaida", role = "aut",
           comment = c(degree = "PhD"))
  )
Description: Provides simulation-based power, type I error, and operating-
    characteristic evaluation for stepped-wedge cluster randomized trials.
    General binary-outcome designs support arbitrary crossover schedules,
    unequal clusters per sequence, multiple baseline periods, secular trends,
    flexible cluster-period sizes, parallel execution with reproducible random-
    number streams, sensitivity grids, and mixed-model diagnostics. Asynchronous
    cumulative-intervention designs allow clusters to transition from control to
    intervention A and later add intervention B at different times, with separate
    wash-in delays, incremental and total treatment contrasts, multiplicity
    adjustment, schedule-identifiability checks, and both conditional and
    failure-aware power. The design and mixed-effects analysis follow Hussey and
    Hughes (2007) <doi:10.1016/j.cct.2006.05.007>. Intraclass correlations for
    binary outcomes are converted to random-intercept standard deviations on the
    latent scale of the logistic-normal model, as reviewed by Eldridge,
    Ukoumunne and Carlin (2009) <doi:10.1111/j.1751-5823.2009.00092.x>. Monte
    Carlo uncertainty is reported using exact intervals from Clopper and Pearson
    (1934) <doi:10.1093/biomet/26.4.404>. An optional comparison with Chen,
    Zhou, Li and Spiegelman (2022) <doi:10.1016/j.cmpb.2021.106522> is also
    provided.
